From 51928680bd1ad6bd84533286df3d01951c09ef46 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mateusz Bajorski <mbajorski@shasta.cloud>
Date: Tue, 26 May 2026 07:35:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] gen-loc-config: PCIe passthrough with deterministic ethN
 naming
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

End-to-end fixes for running EVE + VyOS with PCIe NIC passthrough in
the qemu profile. Several intermediate bugs surfaced and are fixed
here together because they're entangled (a fix at one layer reveals
the next).

A. Passthrough-mode SystemAdapterList. When DeviceIoList contains any
   PhyIoUsageDedicated PCIe entries, NIM treats SystemAdapterList as
   authoritative — anything not listed is invisible to zedrouter and
   any NetworkInstance with a `Port: name` referencing an unlisted
   port fails with "no port is matching label <name>". The previous
   logic listed only the LMP and treated it as the management uplink,
   which (a) lost EVE's actual management uplink so EVE had no
   192.168.1.x address (SSH hostfwd broken) and (b) hid switch-NI
   ports from zedrouter so the inner LAN-bridge NI never activated.

   Fix: when hasPCIe, declare three classes of SystemAdapter:
     * MgmtUplink (profile.mgmt_uplink, Uplink=true, DHCP NetworkUUID)
     * LMP port (Uplink=false, bridge member of LMP NI)
     * Each switch-NI port (Uplink=false, bridge member of its NI)
   Plus matching PhysicalIO entries with Usage=Shared. PCIe adapters
   keep Usage=Dedicated so patch 0016's last-resort filter ignores them.

B. DHCP subnet-id (VyOS 1.5-rolling). service_dhcp-server.py now requires
   a unique subnet-id per shared-network subnet. Without one, boot-time
   load_bootfile fails with the generic "Configuration error". Emit
   `subnet-id '1'` (we only have one subnet).

C. Deterministic guest ethN naming. The cidata template assumes
   eth0=LMP-VIF, eth1=switch-NI-VIF, ethN+=PCIe-passthrough. Empirically
   Linux's driver probe order on this VM puts the e1000e devices at
   eth0/eth1 ahead of the virtio NICs, breaking the assumption.

   VyOS's canonical hw-id udev mechanism doesn't help on first boot
   because vyos_net_name short-circuits after vyos-config_status is
   written — by the time our cidata runs it has long since fired.

   Fix: deliver the cidata as multipart MIME with a `text/cloud-boothook`
   part that runs in cloud-init-local (before vyos-router) and renames
   each kernel ifname by MAC. Two-pass POSIX shell so it can safely
   swap names: first move each interface to a temp `_t<i>_<desired>`,
   then rename each temp to the encoded final. Requires populating
   VyOSConfig.EthHwIds from main() with the LMP/switch-NI VIF MACs +
   the new ProfileInterface.Mac field for PCIe entries.

   VyOS's cloud-init has bootcmd/runcmd stripped from cloud_init_modules
   (per /etc/cloud/cloud.cfg.d/10_vyos.cfg), so a simple bootcmd is a
   no-op. BootHookPartHandler is registered unconditionally and runs at
   user-data consume time, hence the multipart MIME wrapping.

D. Defeat vyos-interface-rescan hw-id corruption. vyos-router calls
   vyos-interface-rescan.py after our cidata applies; it reads
   /run/udev/vyos/<ifname> (cached at coldplug, before our rename) and
   writes config.set([..., ifname, 'hw-id'], <stale-MAC>) over the
   correct hw-id our cidata had just written. interfaces_ethernet
   apply() then calls Interface.set_mac(hw_id) and corrupts the live
   kernel MAC (two interfaces end up with the same MAC).

   Per python/vyos/ifconfig/interface.py:1785-1786 the `mac` directive
   takes precedence over `hw-id` in update(), so emit BOTH
   `set interfaces ethernet ethN mac '<MAC>'` AND `set ... hw-id '<MAC>'`.
   The `mac` survives rescan (rescan only writes hw-id) and is what
   set_mac() ends up applying.

E. profile.MgmtUplink + ProfileInterface.Mac fields. Already covered
   above; mentioned here for changelog completeness.

Verified end-to-end on the qemu profile with E1000E=1:
- Both e1000e devices bound to vfio-pci on EVE, presented as Intel
  82574L inside VyOS at PCI 04:00.0 and 05:00.0.
- VyOS interface names match cidata template:
    eth0 = 02:16:3e:7a:2c:58 (switch NI VIF, br0 member)
    eth1 = 02:16:3e:f0:c1:9b (LMP VIF, 192.168.2.11/24 WAN)
    eth2 = 52:54:00:e1:00:00 (e1000e #1)
    eth3 = 52:54:00:e1:00:01 (e1000e #2)
- No "vyos-config[...]: Configuration error" in logs.
- EVE has 192.168.1.10/24 management uplink; SSH hostfwd works.
- VyOS reachable at 192.168.2.11; LAN bridge br0 has 192.168.33.1/24.

Refs:
- VyOS hw-id mechanism: src/udev/vyos_net_name, src/etc/udev/rules.d/65-vyos-net.rules
- rescan corruption: src/helpers/vyos-interface-rescan.py (config.set hw-id from stale cache)
- mac directive precedence: python/vyos/ifconfig/interface.py:1785-1786
- DHCP subnet-id requirement: src/conf_mode/service_dhcp-server.py (validation error)

Signed-off-by: Mateusz Bajorski <mbajorski@shasta.cloud>
---
 pkg/pillar/cmd/gen-loc-config/main.go | 328 ++++++++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 288 insertions(+), 40 deletions(-)

diff --git a/pkg/pillar/cmd/gen-loc-config/main.go b/pkg/pillar/cmd/gen-loc-config/main.go
index 6cdaceeac..4952341a0 100644
--- a/pkg/pillar/cmd/gen-loc-config/main.go
+++ b/pkg/pillar/cmd/gen-loc-config/main.go
@@ -34,6 +34,7 @@ import (
 	"log"
 	"net"
 	"os"
+	"sort"
 	"strings"
 
 	zconfig "github.com/lf-edge/eve-api/go/config"
@@ -58,6 +59,12 @@ type ProfileInterface struct {
 	Type      string `yaml:"type"`      // "switch" or "pcie"
 	PciLong   string `yaml:"pci"`       // PCI address e.g. "0000:02:00.0" (pcie only)
 	AssignGrp string `yaml:"assigngrp"` // IOMMU group e.g. "group14" (pcie only)
+	// Mac is the MAC address of the device as the guest will see it. For
+	// pcie passthrough this is the NIC's hardware MAC (the device's EEPROM
+	// for real hardware, or QEMU's explicit `mac=` argument for tests).
+	// When set, gen-loc-config will pin a specific guest ethN to this MAC
+	// via VyOS hw-id binding so probe order does not dictate the name.
+	Mac string `yaml:"mac"`
 }
 
 // VyOSConfig describes the VyOS networking to configure via cloud-init.
@@ -79,6 +86,15 @@ type VyOSConfig struct {
 	// EVE's EnforceNetworkInterfaceOrder) plus VyOS's `net.ifnames=0
 	// biosdevname=0` kernel cmdline. Defaults to "eth100" if not set.
 	LMPName string `yaml:"lmp_name"`
+
+	// EthHwIds is populated by main() from the computed VIF MACs (and from
+	// profile.interfaces[].mac for PCIe passthrough devices). The generated
+	// cidata emits `set interfaces ethernet <ethN> hw-id '<mac>'` for each
+	// entry, so VyOS renames the kernel-side ifname to match regardless of
+	// driver-load probe order. Without this, e1000e passthrough devices
+	// often grab eth0/eth1 ahead of the virtio NICs, breaking the cidata's
+	// assumption that the LMP/switch VIFs land at the expected names.
+	EthHwIds map[string]string `yaml:"-"`
 }
 
 // Profile describes a compile-time machine configuration loaded from a YAML file.
@@ -104,6 +120,14 @@ type Profile struct {
 	} `yaml:"network"`
 	// LMP (Local Management Port): bridged to a Switch NI; VM gets eth0 on it.
 	LMP string `yaml:"lmp"`
+	// MgmtUplink is the EVE-side ethN that should be the management uplink
+	// (EVE's outbound network for control plane / fetch / SSH). Used only
+	// when SystemAdapterList must be set explicitly (PCIe-passthrough mode)
+	// because NIM's last-resort DPC otherwise auto-discovers it. For the
+	// QEMU profile this is `eth0` (the first virtio NIC, wired to outer
+	// QEMU's user-net 192.168.1.0/24 with hostfwd 2222→22). For real
+	// hardware, set to whichever physical port has the management network.
+	MgmtUplink string `yaml:"mgmt_uplink"`
 	// Interfaces: additional ports handed to the VM in order (eth1, eth2, …).
 	// type: switch → Switch NI (L2 bridge); type: pcie → PCIe passthrough.
 	Interfaces []ProfileInterface `yaml:"interfaces"`
@@ -140,15 +164,40 @@ func generateVyOSUserData(vyosCfg *VyOSConfig) string {
 		add("set system host-name '" + v.Hostname + "'")
 	}
 
-	// Deterministic ethN naming is guaranteed by two existing mechanisms:
-	//   1. EVE's `EnforceNetworkInterfaceOrder=true` (set unconditionally
-	//      below) makes pciAddressAllocator pin each VIF to a fixed PCI
-	//      bus slot per VifOrder — same VM config → same slots every boot.
-	//   2. VyOS's kernel cmdline carries `net.ifnames=0 biosdevname=0`,
-	//      so the kernel uses legacy ethN names assigned by PCI bus order.
-	// No `set interfaces ethernet <iface> hw-id '<mac>'` is emitted — the
-	// pin above is enough, and adding hw-id added nothing useful while
-	// risking drift if the VM gets re-MAC'd.
+	// Clear all ethernet entries we control, then re-pin each to the right
+	// MAC via hw-id. Reasoning:
+	//   1. The stock small-vyos-ssh-nat image ships baked-in entries for
+	//      eth0/eth1 with address/hw-id/mtu/offload that conflict with our
+	//      bridge-member usage (`address dhcp` on a bridge member is
+	//      rejected — see task #27).
+	//   2. vyos-interface-rescan rewrites entries with the *currently
+	//      observed* kernel MAC at boot. After our boothook renames the
+	//      kernel ifnames, rescan can leave config.boot with an ethN→MAC
+	//      mapping that doesn't match what we intend, and at commit time
+	//      VyOS's interfaces_ethernet apply() FORCES the kernel MAC to
+	//      whatever hw-id says (see vyos-1x interfaces_ethernet.py and
+	//      ifconfig/interface.py:1781-1788). That overrides the actual
+	//      hardware MAC and breaks L2 ('two interfaces with the same MAC').
+	// So: delete every ethN we set up, then re-pin BOTH `mac` and `hw-id`
+	// to the desired MAC. The `mac` directive is the critical one — per
+	// vyos-1x python/vyos/ifconfig/interface.py:1785-1786, `update(config)`
+	// uses `mac` if set, falling back to `hw-id` otherwise. Setting `mac`
+	// makes the live kernel MAC application immune to vyos-interface-rescan
+	// re-writing `hw-id` from its stale /run/udev/vyos/ cache after the
+	// cloud-init boothook rename. `delete` on a missing path is non-fatal.
+	hwKeys := make([]string, 0, len(v.EthHwIds))
+	for k := range v.EthHwIds {
+		hwKeys = append(hwKeys, k)
+	}
+	sort.Strings(hwKeys)
+	for _, ethN := range hwKeys {
+		add("delete interfaces ethernet " + ethN)
+	}
+	for _, ethN := range hwKeys {
+		mac := strings.ToLower(v.EthHwIds[ethN])
+		add("set interfaces ethernet " + ethN + " hw-id '" + mac + "'")
+		add("set interfaces ethernet " + ethN + " mac '" + mac + "'")
+	}
 
 	// WAN interface: DHCP client
 	add("set interfaces ethernet " + v.WAN + " description 'WAN'")
@@ -163,20 +212,6 @@ func generateVyOSUserData(vyosCfg *VyOSConfig) string {
 		bridgeIP := fmt.Sprintf("%s/%d", v.LANGateway, prefix)
 		networkAddr := ipNet.String()
 
-		// Wipe any baked-in ethernet config on each LAN member before
-		// making it a bridge port. The stock small-vyos-ssh-nat image
-		// ships with `interfaces ethernet eth0 { address "dhcp"; hw-id …;
-		// mtu …; offload {…} }`, and VyOS's verify_address rejects a
-		// bridge member that still has an address — the boot-time commit
-		// fails with `Cannot assign address to interface "eth0" as it is
-		// a member of bridge "br0"!` and load_bootfile then prints the
-		// generic `vyos-config[…]: Configuration error`.
-		// `delete` on a path that does not exist is a non-fatal warning,
-		// so this is safe even if a future image stops shipping the node.
-		for _, iface := range v.LAN {
-			add("delete interfaces ethernet " + iface)
-		}
-
 		// LAN bridge
 		add("set interfaces bridge br0 description 'LAN'")
 		add("set interfaces bridge br0 address '" + bridgeIP + "'")
@@ -191,6 +226,12 @@ func generateVyOSUserData(vyosCfg *VyOSConfig) string {
 
 		// DHCP server
 		if v.DHCPStart != "" && v.DHCPStop != "" {
+			// VyOS 1.5-rolling requires `subnet-id` per subnet in dhcp-server
+			// (see service_dhcp-server.py conf_mode validation: "Unique subnet
+			// ID not specified for subnet …"). Without it, the boot-time
+			// commit fails with the generic `vyos-config[…]: Configuration
+			// error`. We only have one subnet so id=1 is fine.
+			add("set service dhcp-server shared-network-name LAN subnet " + networkAddr + " subnet-id '1'")
 			add("set service dhcp-server shared-network-name LAN subnet " + networkAddr + " option default-router '" + v.LANGateway + "'")
 			for _, ns := range v.NameServers {
 				add("set service dhcp-server shared-network-name LAN subnet " + networkAddr + " option name-server '" + ns + "'")
@@ -209,7 +250,89 @@ func generateVyOSUserData(vyosCfg *VyOSConfig) string {
 		add(c)
 	}
 
-	return "#cloud-config\nvyos_config_commands:\n" + strings.Join(cmds, "\n") + "\n"
+	// Build the cloud-config part body. We assemble the multipart MIME
+	// envelope below, since VyOS's cloud-init strips `bootcmd`/`runcmd` from
+	// its module lists (see /etc/cloud/cloud.cfg.d/10_vyos.cfg) — only
+	// growpart, resizefs_vyos, vyos_ifupdown, vyos, write_files,
+	// vyos_userdata, vyos_install fire. To get the ethN rename to run
+	// before vyos-router, we deliver it as a `text/cloud-boothook` MIME
+	// part: cloud-init's BootHookPartHandler is registered unconditionally
+	// (its handler runs at user-data consume time, not via a module), and
+	// the script is executed early in the init stage on every boot.
+	cloudConfig := "#cloud-config\nvyos_config_commands:\n" + strings.Join(cmds, "\n") + "\n"
+
+	// Build the rename script if we have any MAC→ethN bindings to apply.
+	var boothook string
+	if len(v.EthHwIds) > 0 {
+		keys := make([]string, 0, len(v.EthHwIds))
+		for k := range v.EthHwIds {
+			keys = append(keys, k)
+		}
+		sort.Strings(keys)
+		// POSIX-shell rename script. Cloud-init boothooks are written to
+		// /var/lib/cloud/instance/boothooks/ and executed with /bin/sh on
+		// every boot, so no associative arrays / [[ ]] / etc.
+		// Strategy: two passes. Pass 1 finds each desired MAC's current
+		// kernel ifname and renames it to a temp name `_t<i>_<desired>`,
+		// encoding the final name in the temp. Pass 2 walks
+		// /sys/class/net/_t* and renames each temp to the encoded final.
+		// This avoids name collisions when two interfaces need to swap.
+		var b strings.Builder
+		b.WriteString("#cloud-boothook\n")
+		b.WriteString("#!/bin/sh\n")
+		b.WriteString("set -e\n")
+		b.WriteString("i=100\n")
+		for _, eth := range keys {
+			mac := strings.ToLower(v.EthHwIds[eth])
+			fmt.Fprintf(&b, "mac='%s'; desired='%s'\n", mac, eth)
+			b.WriteString("cur=\"\"\n")
+			b.WriteString("for d in /sys/class/net/*; do\n")
+			b.WriteString("    [ -f \"$d/address\" ] || continue\n")
+			b.WriteString("    if [ \"$(cat \"$d/address\")\" = \"$mac\" ]; then\n")
+			b.WriteString("        cur=\"$(basename \"$d\")\"; break\n")
+			b.WriteString("    fi\n")
+			b.WriteString("done\n")
+			b.WriteString("if [ -n \"$cur\" ] && [ \"$cur\" != \"$desired\" ]; then\n")
+			b.WriteString("    ip link set \"$cur\" down 2>/dev/null || true\n")
+			b.WriteString("    ip link set \"$cur\" name \"_t${i}_${desired}\"\n")
+			b.WriteString("    i=$((i+1))\n")
+			b.WriteString("fi\n")
+		}
+		b.WriteString("for d in /sys/class/net/_t*; do\n")
+		b.WriteString("    [ -d \"$d\" ] || continue\n")
+		b.WriteString("    cur=\"$(basename \"$d\")\"\n")
+		b.WriteString("    desired=\"${cur#_t}\"; desired=\"${desired#*_}\"\n")
+		b.WriteString("    ip link set \"$cur\" name \"$desired\"\n")
+		b.WriteString("done\n")
+		boothook = b.String()
+	}
+
+	// If there's nothing to boothook, emit single-part #cloud-config (the
+	// original simple format). Otherwise wrap both parts as multipart MIME
+	// — cloud-init parses the outer Content-Type and dispatches each part
+	// to its handler.
+	if boothook == "" {
+		return cloudConfig
+	}
+	boundary := "===eve-cidata-boundary==="
+	var m strings.Builder
+	m.WriteString("Content-Type: multipart/mixed; boundary=\"" + boundary + "\"\n")
+	m.WriteString("MIME-Version: 1.0\n\n")
+	for _, part := range []struct{ ctype, body string }{
+		{"text/cloud-boothook", boothook},
+		{"text/cloud-config", cloudConfig},
+	} {
+		m.WriteString("--" + boundary + "\n")
+		m.WriteString("Content-Type: " + part.ctype + "; charset=\"us-ascii\"\n")
+		m.WriteString("MIME-Version: 1.0\n")
+		m.WriteString("Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit\n\n")
+		m.WriteString(part.body)
+		if !strings.HasSuffix(part.body, "\n") {
+			m.WriteString("\n")
+		}
+	}
+	m.WriteString("--" + boundary + "--\n")
+	return m.String()
 }
 
 func main() {
@@ -232,6 +355,7 @@ func main() {
 
 	// Profile fields that have no flag equivalents.
 	var lmp string
+	var mgmtUplink string
 	var profileInterfaces []ProfileInterface
 	var vyosCfg *VyOSConfig
 
@@ -255,6 +379,7 @@ func main() {
 		*vncPasswd = p.VNCPasswd
 		*sshKey = p.SSHKey
 		lmp = p.LMP
+		mgmtUplink = p.MgmtUplink
 		profileInterfaces = p.Interfaces
 		vyosCfg = &p.VyOS
 		if p.Output != "" {
@@ -367,13 +492,20 @@ func main() {
 	// Other VirtIO NICs (uplink, switch NIs) follow at InterfaceOrder len(pcieAdapters)+2 onward.
 	var appInterfaces []*zconfig.NetworkAdapter
 
+	// MACs tracked here so we can later map them to the guest ethN names
+	// declared in the VyOSConfig (v.WAN, v.LAN[i]) and emit hw-id pinning
+	// in the cidata. See VyOSConfig.EthHwIds.
+	var lmpMACStr string
+	var switchVIFMACs []string
+
 	if lmp != "" {
 		lmpMAC := lmpMACFromUUIDs(uuid.FromStringOrNil(appUUID), uuid.FromStringOrNil(lmpNIUUID))
+		lmpMACStr = lmpMAC.String()
 		appInterfaces = append(appInterfaces, &zconfig.NetworkAdapter{
 			Name:           "eth0",
 			NetworkId:      lmpNIUUID,
 			InterfaceOrder: 1,
-			MacAddress:     lmpMAC.String(),
+			MacAddress:     lmpMACStr,
 			Acls: []*zconfig.ACE{
 				{
 					Name: "allow-all",
@@ -397,7 +529,7 @@ func main() {
 	// adapterNum allocation:  0 = LMP, 1 = uplink, 2+ = switch NIs (in profile order)
 	virtIntfOrder := uint32(len(pcieAdapters) + 2)
 
-	addVIF := func(niUUID string, adapterNum uint8) {
+	addVIF := func(niUUID string, adapterNum uint8, recordSwitchMAC bool) {
 		vifMAC := vifMACFromUUIDs(uuid.FromStringOrNil(appUUID), uuid.FromStringOrNil(niUUID), adapterNum)
 		ethName := fmt.Sprintf("eth%d", virtIntfOrder-1)
 		appInterfaces = append(appInterfaces, &zconfig.NetworkAdapter{
@@ -406,14 +538,17 @@ func main() {
 			InterfaceOrder: virtIntfOrder,
 			MacAddress:     vifMAC.String(),
 		})
+		if recordSwitchMAC {
+			switchVIFMACs = append(switchVIFMACs, vifMAC.String())
+		}
 		virtIntfOrder++
 	}
 
 	if *uplink != "" {
-		addVIF(natNIUUID, 1)
+		addVIF(natNIUUID, 1, false)
 	}
 	for i, sni := range switchNIs {
-		addVIF(sni.niUUID, uint8(i+2))
+		addVIF(sni.niUUID, uint8(i+2), true)
 	}
 
 	// DeviceIoList: tell EVE about PCIe-assignable adapters.
@@ -443,32 +578,145 @@ func main() {
 	}
 
 	if hasPCIe && lmp != "" {
-		// Add LMP port as a managed PhysicalIO so NIM can build DevicePortConfig for it.
-		lmpNetUUID := uuid.NewV5(ns, *name+"/lmp-network").String()
+		// In passthrough mode SystemAdapterList becomes the authoritative
+		// list of ports NIM manages — anything NOT listed becomes invisible
+		// to zedrouter. We therefore must declare:
+		//   1. The actual management uplink (Uplink=true, with DHCP) — so
+		//      EVE itself has outbound connectivity for control-plane.
+		//      Identity comes from mgmtUplink (the QEMU profile sets
+		//      this to `eth0`, the first virtio NIC = 192.168.1.0/24).
+		//   2. The LMP port (Uplink=false) — used by the LMP Switch NI as
+		//      a non-uplink bridge member. NOT a management uplink despite
+		//      its name.
+		//   3. Each remaining switch-type interface (Uplink=false) — port
+		//      for its Switch NI. Added in the loop below.
+		// PCIe ports are declared with Usage=PhyIoUsageDedicated which keeps
+		// them out of NIM's DPC (per patch 0016).
+		if mgmtUplink == "" {
+			log.Fatalf("hasPCIe + lmp set: profile must declare 'mgmt_uplink:' so EVE's management uplink is preserved (otherwise EVE has no outbound network)")
+		}
+		mgmtNetUUID := uuid.NewV5(ns, *name+"/mgmt-network").String()
 		deviceIoList = append(deviceIoList, &zconfig.PhysicalIO{
 			Ptype:        evecommon.PhyIoType_PhyIoNetEth,
-			Phylabel:     lmp,
-			Logicallabel: lmp,
+			Phylabel:     mgmtUplink,
+			Logicallabel: mgmtUplink,
 			Phyaddrs: map[string]string{
-				"Ifname": lmp,
+				"Ifname": mgmtUplink,
 			},
 			Usage: evecommon.PhyIoMemberUsage_PhyIoUsageMgmtAndApps,
 		})
-		// DHCP network config for the LMP port.
 		networks = append(networks, &zconfig.NetworkConfig{
-			Id:   lmpNetUUID,
+			Id:   mgmtNetUUID,
 			Type: evecommon.NetworkType_V4Only,
 			Ip:   &evecommon.Ipspec{Dhcp: evecommon.DHCPType_Client},
 		})
-		// Tell NIM to manage ONLY the LMP port; PCIe adapters are excluded.
 		systemAdapters = append(systemAdapters, &zconfig.SystemAdapter{
-			Name:           lmp,
+			Name:           mgmtUplink,
 			Uplink:         true,
-			NetworkUUID:    lmpNetUUID,
-			LowerLayerName: lmp,
+			NetworkUUID:    mgmtNetUUID,
+			LowerLayerName: mgmtUplink,
 			Cost:           0,
 		})
-		log.Printf("PCIe passthrough mode: NIM will manage only %s; PCIe adapters excluded from DevicePortConfig", lmp)
+		// LMP port: bridge member of the LMP Switch NI; not an uplink.
+		deviceIoList = append(deviceIoList, &zconfig.PhysicalIO{
+			Ptype:        evecommon.PhyIoType_PhyIoNetEth,
+			Phylabel:     lmp,
+			Logicallabel: lmp,
+			Phyaddrs: map[string]string{
+				"Ifname": lmp,
+			},
+			Usage: evecommon.PhyIoMemberUsage_PhyIoUsageShared,
+		})
+		systemAdapters = append(systemAdapters, &zconfig.SystemAdapter{
+			Name:           lmp,
+			Uplink:         false,
+			LowerLayerName: lmp,
+		})
+		// Switch-NI ports also need to be visible to NIM so zedrouter can
+		// resolve `Port: <name>` on each Switch NetworkInstanceConfig. When
+		// SystemAdapterList is present it's exhaustive — anything not listed
+		// is invisible. Mark each switch port as non-uplink (no mgmt traffic,
+		// no NetworkUUID) and Shared usage in PhysicalIO so NIM still puts
+		// them in DeviceNetworkStatus but doesn't probe them for connectivity.
+		for _, iface := range profileInterfaces {
+			if strings.ToLower(iface.Type) != "switch" {
+				continue
+			}
+			deviceIoList = append(deviceIoList, &zconfig.PhysicalIO{
+				Ptype:        evecommon.PhyIoType_PhyIoNetEth,
+				Phylabel:     iface.Name,
+				Logicallabel: iface.Name,
+				Phyaddrs: map[string]string{
+					"Ifname": iface.Name,
+				},
+				Usage: evecommon.PhyIoMemberUsage_PhyIoUsageShared,
+			})
+			systemAdapters = append(systemAdapters, &zconfig.SystemAdapter{
+				Name:           iface.Name,
+				Uplink:         false,
+				LowerLayerName: iface.Name,
+			})
+		}
+		log.Printf("PCIe passthrough mode: NIM mgmt uplink %s, LMP %s + switch ports as non-uplinks; PCIe adapters excluded from DevicePortConfig", mgmtUplink, lmp)
+	}
+
+	// Build a desired ethN → MAC map covering every interface we want VyOS
+	// to see at a deterministic name, regardless of which driver Linux
+	// probes first. The map is emitted into cidata as a cloud-init
+	// `bootcmd:` block — a tiny shell snippet that swaps kernel ifnames to
+	// match. `bootcmd` runs at every boot in the `cloud-init-local` stage,
+	// which fires before `vyos-router.service` starts, so the rename is in
+	// place before any conf_mode script looks at /sys/class/net.
+	//
+	// Why bootcmd instead of VyOS's canonical hw-id mechanism: VyOS's udev
+	// rename (vyos_net_name) is gated on /run/vyos-config_status, so by the
+	// time cloud-init runs vyos_config_commands the gate is already closed.
+	// hw-id settings made via cidata commit cleanly but never trigger a
+	// rename, so on a fresh first boot the kernel ifnames stay in driver
+	// probe order. With bootcmd we skip the gate and rename directly.
+	if vyosCfg != nil {
+		vyosCfg.EthHwIds = make(map[string]string)
+		// LMP-named guest interface (vyos.lmp_name) gets the LMP NI VIF
+		// MAC. This is the interface that lives on EVE's LMP bridge
+		// (profile.lmp); VyOS configures it as the LAN-side bridge member
+		// and serves DHCP on it. vyos.lan typically lists the same name
+		// (lmp_name == lan[0] is the common single-NI case).
+		if vyosCfg.LMPName != "" && lmpMACStr != "" {
+			vyosCfg.EthHwIds[vyosCfg.LMPName] = lmpMACStr
+		}
+		// Remaining VyOS-managed names (vyos.wan plus any additional
+		// vyos.lan entries) map to switch-NI VIF MACs in profile.interfaces
+		// order. A name already pinned to the LMP MAC above is silently
+		// skipped, so the common lmp_name == lan[0] case is naturally
+		// idempotent.
+		switchIdx := 0
+		assignSwitch := func(name string) {
+			if name == "" || vyosCfg.EthHwIds[name] != "" {
+				return
+			}
+			if switchIdx < len(switchVIFMACs) {
+				vyosCfg.EthHwIds[name] = switchVIFMACs[switchIdx]
+				switchIdx++
+			}
+		}
+		assignSwitch(vyosCfg.WAN)
+		for _, lanEth := range vyosCfg.LAN {
+			assignSwitch(lanEth)
+		}
+		// PCIe passthrough adapters: take the next free ethN beyond what
+		// the VIF assignments above used. profile.interfaces[i].mac must be
+		// set (QEMU runs: explicit `mac=` on the e1000e device line; real
+		// hardware: read from the NIC EEPROM ahead of time).
+		used := len(vyosCfg.EthHwIds)
+		pcieIdx := 0
+		for _, iface := range profileInterfaces {
+			if strings.ToLower(iface.Type) != "pcie" || iface.Mac == "" {
+				continue
+			}
+			eth := fmt.Sprintf("eth%d", used+pcieIdx)
+			vyosCfg.EthHwIds[eth] = iface.Mac
+			pcieIdx++
+		}
 	}
 
 	cloudInitUserData := generateVyOSUserData(vyosCfg)
